Build MozJPEG's Arm Neon SIMD extensions for WebAssembly.
Applies to MozJPEG 4.1.5. Everything above the first "---" is ignored by patch(1).

MozJPEG ships hand-written SIMD in two flavours. The x86 one is NASM assembly
and cannot be assembled for wasm at all. The Arm Neon one is plain C
intrinsics, and Emscripten ships a SIMDe-based <arm_neon.h> that lowers those
intrinsics to wasm SIMD128 - so that flavour can be built, and this patch is
what makes MozJPEG's build system agree to do it.

Three changes:

1. simd/CMakeLists.txt gains an Emscripten branch. Emscripten reports itself
   to CMake as 32-bit x86, so without this the x86 branch is selected and SIMD
   is silently disabled for want of NASM. The new branch names the Neon
   sources explicitly and takes the intrinsics implementation rather than the
   GAS one, which is Arm assembly.

   It uses the AArch32 variants deliberately: __aarch64__ is undefined here,
   so jchuff.h picks a 32-bit bit buffer to match a 32-bit size_t, and the
   #ifdefs inside the shared sources select their AArch32 branches to match.

2. simd/arm/jchuff.h has an SPLAT() that stores four bytes using an inline
   "str" instruction. wasm has no inline assembler, so it takes the portable
   byte-at-a-time store that the MSVC build already uses.

3. simd/arm/aarch32/jsimd.c decides at run time whether Neon is available, by
   reading /proc/cpuinfo. There is no /proc under wasm, and SIMD128 is a
   compile-time target feature in any case: if the translation unit compiled,
   the intrinsics are there. Without this the SIMD kernels build but are never
   dispatched to, which looks exactly like a working build that is mysteriously
   no faster.

--- a/simd/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/simd/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -6,8 +6,46 @@
     set(WITH_SIMD 0 PARENT_SCOPE)
   endif()
 endmacro()
+
+
+###############################################################################
+# WebAssembly (Neon intrinsics via Emscripten's SIMDe-based <arm_neon.h>)
+###############################################################################
+
+# Emscripten reports itself to CMake as a 32-bit x86 target, so the block below
+# would otherwise pick the x86 path and disable SIMD for want of NASM. The x86
+# SIMD extensions are hand-written NASM and cannot be assembled for wasm, but
+# the Neon extensions are plain C intrinsics, and Emscripten ships a SIMDe
+# <arm_neon.h> that lowers them to wasm SIMD128. So we build the Neon sources.
+#
+# We use the AArch32 variants deliberately. __aarch64__ is undefined here, so
+# jchuff.h picks BIT_BUF_SIZE 32, which is what a 32-bit size_t wants, and the
+# per-file #ifdefs inside the shared sources select their AArch32 branches to
+# match. Sizes and code paths are then consistent throughout.
+if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "Emscripten")
+
+set(HAVE_VLD1_S16_X3 1)
+set(HAVE_VLD1_U16_X2 1)
+set(HAVE_VLD1Q_U8_X4 1)
+configure_file(arm/neon-compat.h.in arm/neon-compat.h @ONLY)
+include_directories(${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/arm)
+
+# The GAS implementation is Arm assembly; only the intrinsics are portable.
+set(NEON_INTRINSICS 1 CACHE INTERNAL "" FORCE)
+add_definitions(-DNEON_INTRINSICS)
+
+add_library(simd OBJECT
+  arm/jccolor-neon.c arm/jcgray-neon.c arm/jcphuff-neon.c arm/jcsample-neon.c
+  arm/jdcolor-neon.c arm/jdmerge-neon.c arm/jdsample-neon.c
+  arm/jfdctfst-neon.c arm/jfdctint-neon.c arm/jidctfst-neon.c
+  arm/jidctint-neon.c arm/jidctred-neon.c arm/jquanti-neon.c
+  arm/aarch32/jchuff-neon.c arm/aarch32/jsimd.c)
+
+return()
 
+endif()
 
+
 ###############################################################################
 # x86[-64] (NASM)
 ###############################################################################
--- a/simd/arm/jchuff.h
+++ b/simd/arm/jchuff.h
@@ -74,7 +74,9 @@
 
 #else
 
-#if defined(_MSC_VER) && !defined(__clang__)
+/* wasm has no inline assembler, so it takes the portable byte-at-a-time
+   store below rather than the Arm "str" variant. */
+#if (defined(_MSC_VER) && !defined(__clang__)) || defined(__wasm__)
 #define SPLAT() { \
   buffer[0] = (JOCTET)(put_buffer >> 24); \
   buffer[1] = (JOCTET)(put_buffer >> 16); \
--- a/simd/arm/aarch32/jsimd.c
+++ b/simd/arm/aarch32/jsimd.c
@@ -112,7 +112,10 @@
 
   simd_support = 0;
 
-#if defined(__ARM_NEON__)
+/* wasm SIMD128 is a compile-time target feature, not something to probe for
+   at run time, and there is no /proc/cpuinfo to fall back on. If this
+   translation unit was built at all, the intrinsics are available. */
+#if defined(__ARM_NEON__) || defined(__wasm__)
   simd_support |= JSIMD_NEON;
 #elif defined(__linux__) || defined(ANDROID) || defined(__ANDROID__)
   /* We still have a chance to use Neon regardless of globally used
